" to list everything dated 2026 (which is all of them). Not helpful.\n\nFinal decision: I''ll report based on aggregateInbox, but enrich by doing a targeted search for likely \"attention\" emails to be thorough. I''ll search \"action\" and \"urgent\" together? No, separate queries. Let me just do one: searchMail(\"urgent\"). And searchMail(\"deadline\"). Two parallel calls. If nothing, conclude.\n\nHmm, but if the mailbox has no such emails, these return empty. That''s fine — confirms nothing urgent.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing once more. Perhaps there are multiple mailboxes and aggregateInbox only aggregated ones it could reach. It said mailboxes:1. Let me verify with listMailboxes since I haven''t called it. The instruction says call listMailboxes when you need a mailboxId you don''t have — but for a workspace overview, aggregateInbox handles it. Still, confirming the number of connected mailboxes is useful for the answer (\"across my mailboxes\" — how many?).\n\nLet me call listMailboxes and searchMail in parallel. Good plan:\n- listMailboxes (confirm mailboxes)\n- searchMail for something attention-related\n\nActually the instructions: \"Call listMailboxes before acting on a mailbox you cannot name\" — I''m not acting on a specific one, I''m doing overview. aggregateInbox gave me mailbox name ana@demo.trymetis.email. That''s enough.\n\nI''ll do listMailboxes anyway to confirm \"across my mailboxes\" count, plus a searchMail for \"urgent\". Parallel.\n\nWait, but is there value? aggregateInbox already lists all mailboxes. It said 1. I''ll trust it but a quick confirm is cheap and the user asked across mailboxes plural.\n\nLet me execute: listMailboxes + searchMail(\"action required\"). Actually let me search broader: \"the\" no. Let me search \"review\" (rebrand review) — meh.\n\nI''ll search for \"unread\" no. \n\nLet me searchMail query: \"deadline OR urgent OR action\". Search engines may not support OR. I''ll do \"urgent\".\n\nOK let me just do listMailboxes and searchMail(\"urgent\") in parallel, then conclude.\n\nActually, thinking about efficiency and the instruction to batch independent calls — I''ll do listMailboxes and a couple of searches in one block.\n\nLet me finalize tool calls:\n1. listMailboxes\n2. searchMail(\"urgent\", null)\n3. searchMail(\"action required\", null)\n\nThen based on results, write the answer.\n\nHmm, that might be overkill and return nothing. But it''s fine.\n\nActually, I realize I should also consider that the emails ARE from September and the user might just want a general \"what''s in my inbox\" regardless of exact week. The emails span Sep 22-26, which is a week (Mon Sep 21 - Sun Sep 27, 2026). Interesting! Sep 22-26 is exactly the week of Sep 21-27, 2026. Maybe the demo intends \"this week\" to be that week, and the clock is just mismatched.\n\nSep 22 2026 is a Tuesday. Sep 21 is Monday. So Sep 22-26 is Tue-Fri of that week. This strongly suggests the dataset represents \"this week\" as the week of Sep 21-27, 2026, and the sent-at date (Oct 29) may be a red herring or the demo clock is off.\n\nGiven this, I think the best answer is to present these 5 emails as the week''s mail, noting the dates. The user wants to know what needs attention. Let me analyze:\n\n1. Kestrel Supply Co. — \"This week at the shop\" — Sat Sep 26, read, not starred. → Informational newsletter.\n2. Konkan Bank — \"INR 1,250.00 debited from a/c 4821\" — Fri Sep 25, read. → Bank debit notification. Might want to verify the transaction.\n3. Raj Mehta — \"Invitation: Rebrand review @ Thu Oct 8, 2026 15:00 UTC\" — Thu Sep 24, read. → Calendar invitation for Oct 8. Given \"today\" would be before Oct 8 in Sept timeframe, this needs a response (RSVP).\n4. Brightloom — \"Your order A-42 has shipped\" — Wed Sep 23, read. → Shipping notification.\n5. Codeyard — \"[Codeyard] Your sign-in verification code\" — Tue Sep 22, read. → Verification code, likely expired/no action.\n\nSo what needs attention th