INSERT INTO "mastra_messages" ("id","thread_id","content","role","type","createdAt","resourceId") VALUES ('75044402-405f-4c3c-8c76-2b69799a96d3','9bb9b9e1-1a69-4ecc-8293-23267fd0f2f8','{"format":2,"parts":[{"type":"reasoning","reasoning":"The user pivoted. They asked earlier for unread count; I was paginating. Now a new request: \"Find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\"\n\nShould I still answer the unread count? The new message supersedes. But I had a pending task. The user''s new message changes direction. I should address the new request, and maybe briefly note the unread count status? Hmm. The prior request was not answered. But the user moved on; the polite thing is to answer the new request, and perhaps include the unread count since I nearly have it — but I don''t have the total; it keeps going with more Edge Sender test emails.\n\nActually, they may still want the unread count. But per instructions, answer the latest message. I could give the newsletter results and also a quick note on the unread count if I can complete it. But completing requires more pagination of possibly 200+ Edge Sender emails.\n\nI think the right move: answer the newsletter request now, and add a one-line note that I''ll need more paging to finish the unread count, or offer it. Hmm, but leaving the previous question unanswered is bad. Let me consider: the unread count question — the inbox is full of Edge Sender test emails, many unread. I could estimate: at least 18 + 50 + ... Not accurate.\n\nBetter: focus on the new request. Then offer: \"By the way, on the unread count — the inbox has a large block of unread Edge Sender test messages; I can finish paging to give the exact number.\"\n\nNow for newsletters/digests: search mail for \"digest\", \"newsletter\", and also use labels: `newsletter` label exists. But I don''t have a \"list by label\" tool. Hmm. There''s searchMail. I can search \"digest\" and \"newsletter\". Also identifiable: Kestrel \"This week at Kestrel\", Codeyard weekly digest, Paperstack month, Linkroom, etc.\n\nLet me search: \"digest\", \"weekly\", \"newsletter\", \"this week\". Use searchMail.\n\nAlso listLabels showed a \"newsletter\" label; maybe searchMail supports label query? Not documented. Let me just search subject-ish terms.\n\nI''ll do parallel searches: \"digest\", \"newsletter\", \"this week\", \"weekly\", \"your Paperstack month\" maybe. Let''s do 4-5.\n\nGiven large data, results could be big. Let''s do \"digest\", \"newsletter\", \"this week at\", \"weekly\".\n\nActually \"This week at Kestrel\" emails are numerous (many). They are marketing newsletters. The user asks \"emails that look like newsletters or digests\" — a classification task. Composing a list across the mailbox. Best approach: identify distinct senders/templates.\n\nLet me search.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"The user pivoted. They asked earlier for unread count; I was paginating. Now a new request: \"Find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\"\n\nShould I still answer the unread count? The new message supersedes. But I had a pending task. The user''s new message changes direction. I should address the new request, and maybe briefly note the unread count status? Hmm. The prior request was not answered. But the user moved on; the polite thing is to answer the new request, and perhaps include the unread count since I nearly have it — but I don''t have the total; it keeps going with more Edge Sender test emails.\n\nActually, they may still want the unread count. But per instructions, answer the latest message. I could give the newsletter results and also a quick note on the unread count if I can complete it. But completing requires more pagination of possibly 200+ Edge Sender emails.\n\nI think the right move: answer the newsletter request now, and add a one-line note that I''ll need more paging to finish the unread count, or offer it. Hmm, but leaving the previous question unanswered is bad. Let me consider: the unread count question — the inbox is full of Edge Sender test emails, many unread. I could estimate: at least 18 + 50 + ... Not accurate.\n\nBetter: focus on the new request. Then offer: \"By the way, on the unread count — the inbox has a large block of unread Edge Sender test messages; I can finish paging to give the exact number.\"\n\nNow for newsletters/digests: search mail for \"digest\", \"newsletter\", and also use labels: `newsletter` label exists. But I don''t have a \"list by label\" tool. Hmm. There''s searchMail. I can search \"digest\" and \"newsletter\". Also identifiable: Kestrel \"This week at Kestrel\", Codeyard weekly digest, Paperstack month, Linkroom, etc.\n\nLet me search: \"digest\", \"weekly\", \"newsletter\", \"this week\". Use searchMail.\n\nAlso listLabels showed a \"newsletter\" label; maybe searchMail supports label query? Not documented. Let me just search subject-ish terms.\n\nI''ll do parallel searches: \"digest\", \"newsletter\", \"this week\", \"weekly\", \"your Paperstack month\" maybe. Let''s do 4-5.\n\nGiven large data, results could be big. Let''s do \"digest\", \"newsletter\", \"this week at\", \"weekly\".\n\nActually \"This week at Kestrel\" emails are numerous (many). They are marketing newsletters. The user asks \"emails that look like newsletters or digests\" — a classification task. Composing a list across the mailbox. Best approach: identify distinct senders/templates.\n\nLet me search."}],"createdAt":1793273072298},{"type":"tool-invocation","toolInvocation":{"state":"result","result":{"count":11,"emails":[{"mailboxId":"2509b3b7-2ea9-5861-9415-92dd8cc89134","mailbox":"ana@demo.trymetis.email","id":"WyJJTkJPWCIsIjEwMDAiLDM4Ml0","threadId":"<brz67yl3811n7oj@codeyard.dev>","from":"Codeyard","subject":"Your weekly Codeyard digest","date":"Mon Sep 07 2026 06:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)","isRead":true,"isStarred":false,"snippet":""},{"mailboxId":"2509b3b7-2ea9-5861-9415-92dd8cc89134","mailbox":"ana@demo.trymetis.email","id